/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-6","number":6,"title":"MIDI Editor","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"6.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-6-1-101","code":"6.1","title":"6.1","blurb":"6.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l6-1-1","title":"6.1.1 — Open editor by tapping Clip Preview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.1 — Open editor by tapping Clip Preview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the MIDI Editor from the Clip Preview so you can see every captured note on a grid instead of only the mini preview."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note’s Instrument View keeps device parameters at the top. Under those tabs sits the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> window: a compact strip that shows the notes already stored in the current clip. That strip is not decoration. Tap it and the MIDI Editor opens in place of the compact preview."},{"type":"prose","html":"The editor is still part of Instrument View. Pads stay on screen when the editor is beside them. You get a time ruler, a loop brace, and a grid of rows (drum hits or pitches) and columns (timeline positions). From here you add, move, copy, or delete notes by hand instead of recapturing the whole take."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ states the same path in shorter language: access the MIDI Editor through Instrument View, either beside the pads or later in full screen. A long-press on the preview is a different gesture. That press opens loop options (duplicate, crop, delete, or create a clip). A short tap is what opens the editor."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when a Capture is close but not right: a kick one cell late, a bass note too long, a hat you never meant to hit. The editor is the repair room for clips you already captured."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set and enter Instrument View from an existing clip (double-tap the clip, or tap the track header).","Confirm you can see the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> strip under the Track Preset tabs.","Tap the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> once. Do not long-press.","Confirm the MIDI Editor grid, time ruler, and loop brace replace the compact preview.","Play the clip and watch the playhead move across the notes you captured.","Leave the Set, come back to the same clip, and open the editor from memory without reading this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What single gesture opens the MIDI Editor from Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> A tap on the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> window.","How is that different from a long-press on the same strip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Long-press opens loop options (duplicate, crop, delete, or create a clip). Tap opens the editor.","Does the editor leave Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is part of Instrument View. Pads can remain visible beside it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 Instrument View; §8.2 MIDI Editor","Note FAQ, “How can I access the MIDI Editor?”","Capture in Note, Editing Notes (tap Clip Mini Preview to open Clip View)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open three different clips — one Drum Rack, one Drift or Wavetable, one Melodic Sampler. Tap each <strong>Clip Preview</strong> and name what you see on the first row of the grid. Close and reopen each editor once without looking at this page."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-2","title":"6.1.2 — Beside-pads view: rows with notes (or selected pad rows)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.2 — Beside-pads view: rows with notes (or selected pad rows)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read the compact MIDI Editor that sits beside the pads and know why it hides empty rows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the MIDI Editor shares the screen with Pad View, Note does not draw every possible pitch or pad. It shows only the rows that already contain notes. If the clip is empty, it shows only the rows that belong to the pad or piano key you currently have selected."},{"type":"prose","html":"That rule keeps the beside-pads editor short enough to leave room for playing. A four-hit drum loop shows four rows, not all sixteen pads. A two-note bass riff shows two pitch rows, not a full keyboard. Select a different empty pad on a blank clip and the visible row follows that pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"Full screen is the opposite choice. Expanding the editor reveals every row in the selected key range, or every Drum Rack pad row. Use beside-pads view when you are still playing and only need to see what you already put in the clip. Expand when you need to add a note on a row that is not visible yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not treat a missing row as a missing sound. The sample or pitch is still on the instrument. The compact editor simply refuses to spend vertical space on silent rows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drum Rack clip that already has kick, snare, and hat notes.","Tap the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> so the editor sits above the 4×4 pads.","Count the visible rows. Confirm they match only the pads that have notes.","Create an empty clip on the same track. Select one unused pad, then open the editor.","Confirm the empty editor shows only the selected pad’s row.","Repeat the empty-clip test on a melodic track: select one key, open the editor, and confirm only that pitch row appears."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In beside-pads view, which rows appear in a clip that already has notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> Only the rows that already contain notes.","What does the compact editor show when the clip has no notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> Only the rows for the currently selected pad or piano key.","Where do you go to see every Drum Rack pad row at once? <strong>Answer:</strong> Full-screen MIDI Editor (Expand)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (beside Pad View row rule; full-screen shows all rows)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a one-bar drum loop that uses only kick and closed hat. Open the beside-pads editor and write down how many rows you see. Add a snare by playing the pad, then reopen the editor and confirm a third row appeared."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-3","title":"6.1.3 — Expand full screen","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.3 — Expand full screen","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expand the MIDI Editor to full screen so every pitch or drum-pad row is visible for detailed editing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Beside the pads, the editor is a slice of Instrument View. Tap the <strong>Expand</strong> button and the grid takes the screen. Full screen shows all rows in the selected key range on a melodic clip, or all drum-pad rows in a Drum Rack. Empty rows come back. That is the view you need when you want to draw a note the compact editor was hiding."},{"type":"prose","html":"Full screen also unlocks a bottom menu that the compact view does not show: <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong>, the note-editing menu icon, <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>, and <strong>Triplets</strong>. You can rotate the device into landscape while expanded. Those extras are why Expand is not just “make it bigger.” It is a different working mode."},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Expand</strong> button itself can sit in any corner if it covers notes. Drag it. Collapse full screen before you close the editor by tapping a Track Preset tab. If you leave Instrument View while expanded, Note remembers that state the next time you open a track."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use full screen when you are repairing a take, drawing a new line, or checking a 16-bar clip. Stay beside the pads when you still need to play."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip and tap the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> to enter the compact MIDI Editor.","Find the <strong>Expand</strong> button and tap it.","Confirm pads leave the screen and every drum-pad row or key-range row is visible.","Look at the bottom menu and name the four extra controls: <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong>, note menu, <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>, <strong>Triplets</strong>.","Collapse full screen, then expand again from memory.","Play the clip in full screen and confirm you can still hear it while you watch the grid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does full screen show that beside-pads view hides? <strong>Answer:</strong> All rows in the selected key range, or all Drum Rack pad rows — including empty ones.","Name one control that appears only after Expand. <strong>Answer:</strong> Any of: <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong>, note-editing menu icon, <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>, <strong>Triplets</strong>.","Must you stop the clip before expanding? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The sources describe Expand as a view change, not a transport stop."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (Expand; full-screen rows; bottom menu)","Note FAQ, MIDI Editor access (alongside pads or full-screen)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expand a Drum Rack clip and a melodic clip. On each, add one note on a row that was invisible in beside-pads view. Collapse, reopen compact view, and confirm that new row now appears because it contains a note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-4","title":"6.1.4 — Move Expand button to any corner","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.4 — Move Expand button to any corner","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drag the <strong>Expand</strong> button to a different MIDI Editor corner so it stops covering the notes you need to edit."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Expand</strong> button sits on the MIDI Editor so you can jump to full screen. On a phone, that control can land on top of a high hat row or a high melody note. The manual’s fix is direct: tap and drag the button to any of the editor’s four corners."},{"type":"prose","html":"You are not changing a preference page. You are moving a handle that was in the way. Put it on the opposite corner from the notes you are drawing. If you later expand to full screen, the same idea applies — keep the control off the region you are pinching or selecting."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is a small layout skill, but it matters the first time you try to tap a note that lives under the button and accidentally expand instead. Treat the button as movable chrome, not as a fixed logo."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the MIDI Editor on a clip that has notes in more than one corner of the grid.","Tap and hold the <strong>Expand</strong> button, then drag it to the opposite corner.","Confirm the button stays in the new corner and the notes it was covering are tappable.","Drag it to each remaining corner once.","Leave it in the corner that leaves your busiest rows clear.","Expand and collapse once to confirm you can still find the button."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you relocate the <strong>Expand</strong> button? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap and drag it to the desired corner of the MIDI Editor.","How many corners can it occupy? <strong>Answer:</strong> Any of the MIDI Editor’s corners.","Why move it? <strong>Answer:</strong> So it does not sit on top of notes you need to tap or draw."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (Expand button can be moved to any corner)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open a busy drum clip. Move <strong>Expand</strong> to the corner farthest from the kick row, add or delete one kick, then move the button back. Repeat on a high melody note so you feel the same conflict on a melodic grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-5","title":"6.1.5 — Full-screen bottom menu: Undo/Redo, note menu, Duplicate Loop, Triplets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.5 — Full-screen bottom menu: Undo/Redo, note menu, Duplicate Loop, Triplets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the four extra controls that appear only when the MIDI Editor is expanded to full screen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Compact editor lives above the pads. Full screen replaces that layout with a dedicated bottom menu. The manual lists four items there:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong> arrows — reverse or restore edits you just made in the Set.","The note-editing menu icon — opens the same property tabs you get when a selection exists (<strong>Operations</strong>, <strong>Transpose</strong>, <strong>Quantization</strong>, <strong>Velocity</strong>, plus <strong>Pitches</strong> on drums).","<strong>Duplicate Loop</strong> — copies the current loop. This is the full-screen name for the loop-duplication action you also reach by long-pressing the loop brace when loop length equals clip length.","<strong>Triplets</strong> — changes the editor’s grid spacing to triplets so you can place and see swung-subdivision notes on the lines instead of between them."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"These controls are why Expand is a working mode, not a zoom. You undo a bad delete without leaving the grid. You open the note menu without first hunting for a selection handle. You double a two-bar idea without returning to Session View. You switch the grid to triplets before you draw a 1/8T hat figure."},{"type":"prose","html":"Instrument View still has its own <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong> and <strong>Note Quantize</strong> button when pads are visible. Full screen puts the editor-centric versions under the grid so your thumbs stay on the notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that already contains a short loop and tap <strong>Expand</strong>.","Point to each bottom-menu control and name it: <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong>, note menu, <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>, <strong>Triplets</strong>.","Tap <strong>Triplets</strong> and confirm the grid spacing changes. Tap it again to restore the straight grid.","Open the note-editing menu from the icon, then close it.","Tap <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong> once and listen. Confirm the loop is longer and the original figure repeats.","Use <strong>Undo</strong> to reverse that duplicate, then <strong>Redo</strong> to bring it back. Leave the clip in the state you want."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does the bottom menu with those four items appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> Only when the MIDI Editor is expanded to full screen.","What does <strong>Triplets</strong> change in that menu? <strong>Answer:</strong> The editor’s grid spacing, to triplets.","What does <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It duplicates the current loop (same family of action as the brace’s <strong>Duplicate</strong>)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (full-screen menu: Undo/Redo, note editing menu icon, Duplicate Loop, Triplets)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.4 (Duplicate when loop length equals clip length)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expand a 1-bar drum clip. Turn <strong>Triplets</strong> on, add one triplet hat, turn <strong>Triplets</strong> off, then <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong> to two bars. Undo once and confirm the extra bar disappears."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-6","title":"6.1.6 — Landscape full screen","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.6 — Landscape full screen","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Rotate the device while the MIDI Editor is expanded so a longer stretch of the timeline fits on screen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Full-screen MIDI Editor is the only documented layout that you can use in landscape. The manual shows this on iPhone: expand first, then turn the device. The grid becomes wider. More beats fit between the left and right edges, which is the point of the rotation."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beside-pads view stays in the normal Instrument View stack — parameters, preview, pads — so landscape is not the tool for playing pads. Landscape is the tool for reading a long clip, drawing a phrase that crosses several bars, or checking a 16-bar maximum loop without pinching as hard."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still pinch to zoom and swipe to move along the timeline. Landscape does not raise the 16-bar clip limit. It only gives the same clip more horizontal pixels. Collapse to portrait when you need the pads again."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the <strong>Expand</strong> button covers a note after you rotate, drag it to another corner. The corner you chose in portrait may sit on a different part of the grid in landscape."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that is at least four bars long. Two bars is acceptable if that is all you have; add empty length later if needed.","Tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong>, then tap <strong>Expand</strong>.","Rotate the device to landscape.","Confirm the editor remains full screen and the timeline is wider than it was in portrait.","Swipe the timeline left and right. Pinch out on a one-bar region, then pinch back in.","Rotate back to portrait, collapse Expand, and return to the pads."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What must be true before landscape editing is available? <strong>Answer:</strong> The MIDI Editor must be in full screen.","Does landscape raise the clip-length limit? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Clips still max out at 16 bars. Landscape only widens the view.","Is landscape the view for playing the 4×4 or 5×5 pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Pads belong to the non-expanded Instrument View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (full screen in landscape; iPhone example)","Note Release Notes 1.3 (landscape when expanded)","Note FAQ (clips maximum 16 bars)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expand a clip, rotate to landscape, and count how many bars you can see without swiping. Pinch in until the whole clip fits, write that number down, then return to portrait and compare."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-7","title":"6.1.7 — Pinch zoom; swipe timeline","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.7 — Pinch zoom; swipe timeline","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pinch the MIDI Editor grid to zoom, then swipe the timeline so you can edit a single cell or survey the whole clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The MIDI Editor grid is musical time, not a fixed picture. Two fingers, pinch out, and columns get wider. Individual 16th-note cells become large enough to tap without hitting the neighbor. Pinch in and the same clip shrinks so more bars fit on screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"After you zoom in, the clip is wider than the display. Swipe left or right to walk the timeline. You are not changing loop length when you swipe. You are only moving the camera. Loop length still belongs to the loop brace at the top."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use pinch-out when you need to grab a note’s right edge for length, or the velocity handle near that edge. Use pinch-in when you want to see whether a fill sits in bar 4 or bar 8. The loop brace has its own zoom gestures (swipe up to zoom to the loop, swipe down to zoom out to the clip). Those come in the next module. This lesson is only the two-finger pinch on the grid plus the left/right swipe."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.3 is the release that documents pinch zoom with the rest of the editor feature set. The local manual describes the same gestures without a version number. If your install predates 1.3, the grid may not pinch; update Note before you treat a missing pinch as a broken clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the MIDI Editor on a clip that is at least two bars long.","Place two fingers on the grid and pinch out until a single beat is obviously larger.","Swipe left, then right, and confirm you are scrolling time rather than moving notes.","Pinch in until the whole clip is visible again.","Zoom into one late note, tap it, and decide whether it should stay.","Pinch back to the full clip and listen once."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which gesture zooms the MIDI Editor grid? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two-finger pinch out to zoom in; pinch in to zoom out.","After you zoom in, how do you move along the clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Swipe left and right on the grid.","Does a timeline swipe change the loop length? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Swiping navigates. The loop brace sets loop length."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (pinch out / pinch in; swipe timeline)","Note Release Notes 1.3 (pinch zoom)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Zoom into bar 1 of a drum clip and delete one extra hat. Zoom out, confirm the rest of the clip is untouched, then zoom into the last bar and add one note. Listen to the whole loop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-8","title":"6.1.8 — Close editor via Track Preset tabs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.8 — Close editor via Track Preset tabs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Close the MIDI Editor by collapsing full screen (if it is open) and tapping a Track Preset tab so the device knobs return."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The MIDI Editor does not have a separate “Done” page in the manual. You close it from the Track Preset tabs that sit above the grid — the same tabs that hold the instrument, Sample, and the two effect slots."},{"type":"prose","html":"Order matters. If you are in full screen, collapse that mode first. Then tap any Track Preset tab. The editor goes away and the parameter knobs come back. Tapping a tab while still expanded is the wrong first move; collapse, then tap."},{"type":"prose","html":"Closing the editor also clears any note selection. The manual states that selection is cleared whenever you close the MIDI Editor or leave Instrument View. If you had a multi-note selection and a property menu open, that menu goes with the editor."},{"type":"prose","html":"This close path is how you bounce between “fix the notes” and “shape the sound” without returning to Session View. Edit a late snare, tap the first tab, tweak <strong>Decay</strong>, tap the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> again, and you are back on the grid."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip, tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong>, and make a small edit so you know you were in the editor.","If you are in full screen, tap <strong>Expand</strong> again (or the collapse control) so the editor is no longer full screen.","Tap any Track Preset tab above the editor.","Confirm the MIDI Editor is gone and device or effect parameters are visible.","Tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong> to reopen the editor, expand to full screen, then close it using the collapse-then-tab sequence.","Repeat the close from memory on a second track."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do you tap to close the MIDI Editor? <strong>Answer:</strong> Any of the <strong>Track Preset</strong> tabs above the editor, after collapsing full screen if it is open.","What happens to a note selection when the editor closes? <strong>Answer:</strong> The selection is cleared.","Do you need to return to Session View to leave the editor? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Closing via the Track Preset tabs keeps you in Instrument View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (close: collapse full screen, tap Track Preset tabs)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.2 (selection clears when you close the editor or exit Instrument View)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one clip, cycle five times: open editor → add or delete one note → close via a Track Preset tab → tweak one knob → reopen. Stop when the open/close path is automatic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-9","title":"6.1.9 — Editor state persists when leaving Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.9 — Editor state persists when leaving Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Instrument View with the MIDI Editor open and confirm Note restores that same editor state when you come back."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The MIDI Editor is sticky. If it is open when you tap the back arrow to Session View, the next visit to Instrument View reopens the editor in the same state you left. The manual’s example is explicit: expand to full screen, press the back arrow in the upper-left corner, then open a track again — you see the expanded MIDI Editor, not the pad-first Instrument View."},{"type":"prose","html":"That persistence is a workflow feature, not a crash. Use it when you are bouncing between Session launches and note repair. Launch a scene, jump into a clip, fix one note, jump out, launch the next scene. You do not have to tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong> every time."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same rule clears note selection when you exit Instrument View. The grid can come back expanded, but a multi-note selection does not survive the trip. Re-select after you return."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not confuse this with auto-save of the Set. Auto-save writes the project every 10–15 seconds. Editor-view persistence is only about which Instrument View layout greets you when you reopen a track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip, tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong>, then tap <strong>Expand</strong> so the editor is full screen.","Tap the back arrow to return to Session View. Do not close the editor first.","Open the same track again (track header or the same clip).","Confirm the MIDI Editor is still open and still expanded.","Collapse to the compact editor, return to Session View, and reopen the track.","Confirm the editor comes back compact, matching the state you left."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What happens if you leave Instrument View while the MIDI Editor is open? <strong>Answer:</strong> The next time you enter Instrument View, the MIDI Editor opens in the same state.","Does a note selection survive that trip? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Selection is cleared when you exit Instrument View.","Give the manual’s full-screen example. <strong>Answer:</strong> Expand, tap the back arrow to Session View, open a track again, and the expanded editor is waiting."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (state restored on return to Instrument View)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.2 (selection clears on close or exit)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Edit one clip in full-screen landscape. Jump to Session View, launch the clip, jump back. Confirm you land in the expanded editor and can keep editing without tapping <strong>Clip Preview</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-1-10","title":"6.1.10 — Persistent view state across tracks","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.10 — Persistent view state across tracks","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.1 — Editor layouts</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Test whether an open MIDI Editor comes back when you open a <em>different</em> track, and record what your installed version actually does."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual’s persistence paragraph is about leaving Instrument View and coming back. The example wording is “the next time you open a track, you will see the expanded MIDI Editor.” That sentence does not say “the same track only.” It also does not name a 1.4 version gate."},{"type":"prose","html":"The local Note Release Notes extract lists 1.4 as Auto Filter and more pads on larger screens. It does <strong>not</strong> mention cross-track editor state. The syllabus stamps this lesson “1.4+”. Treat that stamp as unsourced in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"So you test, you do not invent. Open track A with the editor expanded. Return to Session View. Open track B. Write down whether you land in the expanded editor, the compact editor, or pad-first Instrument View. Then open track A again. That observation is the lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"Whatever you see, selection still clears when you leave Instrument View. Track Preset tabs still close the editor on the track you are looking at. Persistence is a view memory, not a shared selection across clips."},{"type":"prose","html":"If two tracks behave differently — drums vs melodic, empty clip vs full clip — write that down too. Do not generalize past the trial on your device."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On track A, open the MIDI Editor and expand it to full screen.","Return to Session View with the editor still open.","Open a different track (track B) from its header or a clip.","Write one line: expanded editor / compact editor / pads only.","Return to Session View and reopen track A. Write what you see.","Repeat once with the compact (not expanded) editor on track A."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the manual actually promise? <strong>Answer:</strong> If the MIDI Editor is open when you leave Instrument View, it reopens in the same state the next time you go to Instrument View. The example says “open a track.”","Do the local release notes document a 1.4 cross-track editor feature? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> — RN 1.4 in the extract is Auto Filter and more pads.","What always clears when you exit Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note selection."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor (return-to-Instrument-View state)","Note Release Notes 1.4 (no editor-persistence line in the local extract)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Run the A/B track test twice, once drums-to-synth and once synth-to-drums. Keep the written results in the Set name or a note so later lessons do not assume a behavior you did not see."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-1-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-6-2-102","code":"6.2","title":"6.2","blurb":"6.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l6-2-1","title":"6.2.1 — Time ruler and loop brace","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.1 — Time ruler and loop brace","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read the MIDI Editor’s time ruler and loop brace so you can see clip length and the looping region as two different things."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The MIDI Editor’s top edge is a time ruler plus a <strong>loop brace</strong>. Columns under that ruler are timeline positions. The brace is the region that repeats when the clip plays. You can change loop size without throwing the rest of the clip away."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the clip is longer than one bar, the brace shows grid lines on each bar. Tap a bar to add it to the loop or remove it. Drag an edge of the brace left or right after a tap-and-hold to resize the loop continuously. Those two gestures — tap a bar, drag an edge — are how you sculpt the repeating window."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clip length and loop length are not required to match. A four-bar capture can loop only bars 2–3. The notes outside the brace stay in the clip until you <strong>Crop</strong> or delete them. That is how you audition a tighter hook without destroying the take."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clips max out at 16 bars (FAQ and Note 1.0.15). The ruler will not grow past that. Uneven lengths — two bars on drums, four on a pad — are legal from 1.0.15 onward."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the MIDI Editor on a clip that is at least two bars long.","Point to the time ruler and to the <strong>loop brace</strong> that sits on it.","If the clip is longer than one bar, tap one bar inside the brace to remove it from the loop, then tap it again to put it back.","Tap and hold a brace edge and drag it one bar shorter. Play. Confirm only the braced region repeats.","Drag the edge back so loop length matches clip length again.","Close the editor and reopen it. Confirm the brace is where you left it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two things sit at the top of the MIDI Editor? <strong>Answer:</strong> A time ruler and a loop brace.","How do you add or remove a whole bar from the loop when the clip is longer than one bar? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap that bar on the loop brace.","If the loop is shorter than the clip, what happens to notes outside the brace? <strong>Answer:</strong> They stay in the clip until you crop or delete them. They just do not play in the loop."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 MIDI Editor; §8.2.4 Editing Clip and Loop Length","Note FAQ, maximum clip length 16 bars","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars; uneven clip lengths)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one clip, set the brace to loop only the last bar. Listen. Restore a two-bar loop. Do not crop yet — that is a later lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-2","title":"6.2.2 — Long-press brace: duplicate / crop / delete loop","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.2 — Long-press brace: duplicate / crop / delete loop","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press the loop brace and choose <strong>Duplicate</strong>, <strong>Crop</strong>, <strong>Delete</strong>, <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong>, or <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong> based on whether the loop fills the whole clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap and hold the loop brace to open its options. Which options you get depends on loop length versus clip length."},{"type":"prose","html":"When loop length <strong>equals</strong> clip length, the menu offers <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. <strong>Duplicate</strong> copies the loop (the clip grows by another copy of itself). <strong>Delete</strong> deletes the clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"When loop length is <strong>shorter</strong> than clip length, two more options appear: <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong> (fit the braced region on screen) and <strong>Crop</strong> (make the clip as long as the loop and discard everything outside the brace). <strong>Delete</strong> also changes meaning. It no longer deletes the whole clip. It deletes only the content inside the brace and leaves the rest of the clip in place."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you have pinched in, the same long-press can show <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong>, which jumps the view back to the entire clip. Note 1.3 documents loop-brace duplicate/crop with the rest of the editor pack."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick the command by intention. Duplicate to turn a 2-bar hook into 4 bars you can vary. Crop to commit a shorter loop and throw away the slop. Delete-inside-the-brace to punch a hole without killing the clip. Zoom commands change only the camera."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a 2-bar clip whose brace matches the full clip. Long-press the brace.","Confirm you see <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. Duplicate once, listen, then <strong>Undo</strong>.","Shorten the brace to one bar. Long-press again.","Confirm <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong> and <strong>Crop</strong> are now available, and that <strong>Delete</strong> is no longer “delete the clip.”","Choose <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong>, then long-press and choose <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong> if it appears.","Crop only if you are willing to discard the unused bar. Otherwise <strong>Undo</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When are <strong>Duplicate</strong> and clip-level <strong>Delete</strong> available? <strong>Answer:</strong> When loop length equals clip length.","When do <strong>Crop</strong> and <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong> appear, and what does <strong>Delete</strong> do then? <strong>Answer:</strong> When the loop is shorter than the clip. <strong>Delete</strong> then removes only the contents of the brace.","What does <strong>Crop</strong> discard? <strong>Answer:</strong> Content outside the loop brace. Clip length becomes the loop length."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.4 Editing Clip and Loop Length","Note Release Notes 1.3 (loop brace duplicate/crop)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a 4-bar Free Length-style clip (or extend a 2-bar idea with Duplicate). Shorten the brace to the best two bars, listen, then Crop. You now have a committed 2-bar loop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-3","title":"6.2.3 — Double Loop / Duplicate Loop naming","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.3 — Double Loop / Duplicate Loop naming","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the documented <strong>Duplicate</strong> / <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong> commands and treat any on-screen “Double Loop” label as something to record, not to invent."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The local extracts name this action two ways, both official:"},{"type":"list","items":["Full-screen bottom menu: <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>.","Loop-brace long-press (when loop length equals clip length): <strong>Duplicate</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Both copy the current loop so the clip grows by another statement of the same material. A 2-bar idea becomes 4 bars. A 1-bar drum figure becomes 2. After the duplicate, you can edit the second half without losing the first."},{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus title also says “Double Loop.” That string does <strong>not</strong> appear in the Ableton Note Manual extract or in the condensed release notes. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for the official name “Double Loop.” If your installed Note shows that wording on a button, write down the version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and use the label you see. Do not assume a third command exists beside <strong>Duplicate</strong> / <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musical reason: duplication is how you turn a captured hook into a section you can vary. Change velocity or drop a note in bars 3–4. Keep bars 1–2 as the original."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clips still stop at 16 bars. Duplicating an 8-bar clip to 16 is legal. Duplicating a 16-bar clip further is not."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a 1- or 2-bar clip whose loop brace matches the clip length.","Expand to full screen and tap <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>. Listen. Confirm the figure now plays twice.","<strong>Undo</strong>. Long-press the brace and choose <strong>Duplicate</strong>. Confirm you get the same kind of result.","Look at every label you just used. Write the exact words on the buttons.","If you see “Double Loop” anywhere, record it next to your Note version. If you do not, record that too.","Leave the clip at a useful doubled length (2 or 4 bars) for later editing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the full-screen control called in the manual? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong>.","What is the brace-menu control called when loop equals clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Duplicate</strong>.","Is “Double Loop” documented in the local extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. Use the on-screen label if it appears, and keep the version number."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 (Duplicate Loop icon); §8.2.4 (Duplicate on the loop brace)","Note Release Notes 1.3 (loop brace duplicate/crop)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate a 2-bar bass clip to 4 bars. Change one note only in the second half. Play the 4-bar loop and confirm the first two bars are untouched."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-4","title":"6.2.4 — Swipe up brace zoom to loop; swipe down zoom out","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.4 — Swipe up brace zoom to loop; swipe down zoom out","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Zoom the MIDI Editor to the loop or back out to the whole clip by swiping on the loop brace."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You already know two-finger pinch on the grid. The loop brace has its own zoom gestures. Swipe <strong>up</strong> on the brace to zoom to the loop — the editor frames whatever the brace currently covers. Swipe <strong>down</strong> to zoom out to the clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"These match the long-press commands <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong> and <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong>, but they do not open a menu. Use them when the loop is shorter than the clip and you want to work inside the repeating region without staring at silent bars outside the brace."},{"type":"prose","html":"Swipe-up is a camera move. It does not crop. Notes outside the brace remain in the clip. If you need to commit the shorter length, that is still <strong>Crop</strong> from the long-press menu."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.3 lists “swipe loop brace zoom” with the rest of the editor pack. If swipe-up does nothing, check that you are on the brace, not on the note grid, and that your Note version includes 1.3."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that is longer than its loop (for example a 4-bar clip with a 2-bar brace).","Swipe up on the <strong>loop brace</strong>. Confirm the editor zooms to fit the loop.","Swipe down on the brace. Confirm you see the whole clip again, including content outside the loop.","Pinch the grid so you are zoomed into one beat, then long-press the brace and use <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong> if you want the menu equivalent.","Swipe up again and add or delete one note inside the loop only.","Swipe down and play the clip. Confirm notes outside the brace are still there."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which swipe zooms to the loop? <strong>Answer:</strong> Swipe up on the loop brace.","Which swipe zooms out to the clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Swipe down on the loop brace.","Does swipe-up crop the clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only changes the zoom. <strong>Crop</strong> is a separate long-press command."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.4 (swipe up zoom to loop; swipe down zoom out to clip; Zoom to Loop / Zoom to Clip)","Note Release Notes 1.3 (swipe loop brace zoom)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a 4-bar clip, set the brace to bars 2–3. Swipe up, tidy those two bars, swipe down, and decide whether to Crop or keep the outer bars as alternate material."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-5","title":"6.2.5 — Uneven clip lengths","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.5 — Uneven clip lengths","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a scene whose clips are not all the same length, and hear how the longer clip sets how long that scene plays when you export."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"From Note 1.0.15, clips may have uneven lengths. A 1-bar drum loop can sit next to a 4-bar pad and an 8-bar vocal-style sampler phrase. You are not required to duplicate every clip to the longest length just to make Session View look even."},{"type":"prose","html":"Each clip still loops on its own length when you launch it. A 1-bar hat figure repeats four times while a 4-bar chord clip plays once. That is the musical point: ostinatos stay short; progressions stay long."},{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus stamps this “1.0.16+”. The local release notes place uneven lengths in <strong>1.0.15</strong>, the same drop that raised the clip cap to 16 bars. Teach 1.0.15 from the extract. If your Help screen shows an older build, update before you treat equal lengths as a law."},{"type":"prose","html":"Export still has a scene rule: scenes render top to bottom, and the <strong>longest clip in a scene</strong> sets that scene’s length in the bounced file. Uneven clips are legal. They just make the longest one the ruler at export time."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm your Note version in <strong>Settings → Help</strong> is 1.0.15 or later.","Make a 1-bar drum clip and a 4-bar melodic clip in the same scene.","Launch the scene. Confirm the drums loop four times against one pass of the melody.","Optionally add an 8-bar clip in another track of the same scene.","Look at Session View. Accept that the clip blocks are different lengths.","Play the scene once more and count drum cycles against the long clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which release first documents uneven clip lengths in the local notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.15 (syllabus 1.0.16 is not in the extract).","Do all clips in a scene have to match length? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Uneven lengths are allowed.","What sets a scene’s length in an audio export? <strong>Answer:</strong> The longest clip in that scene."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars; uneven clip lengths)","Note FAQ (maximum clip length 16 bars)","Ableton Note Manual §5.4 / export rule via (longest clip in a scene)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build one scene with a 1-bar kick/hat loop, a 2-bar bass, and a 4-bar pad. Do not duplicate the short clips. Listen until you can tap the 4-bar downbeat from memory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-6","title":"6.2.6 — Up to 16 bars","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.6 — Up to 16 bars","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat 16 bars as the hard clip-length ceiling and choose Fixed Length values that respect it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note clips are at most 16 bars long. The FAQ states the limit in one sentence. Release notes 1.0.15 raised clips to that ceiling (and allowed uneven lengths). Fixed Length capture offers 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars when you tap the plus control above the pads — the manual’s list includes 16."},{"type":"prose","html":"You cannot make a 32-bar verse inside one clip. If the idea is longer, you split it across scenes (max 8) or across clips in the same track. Duplicate Loop will grow a clip only until it hits the ceiling."},{"type":"prose","html":"Free Length with transport stopped records up to eight bars before Capture. That is a capture-buffer limit, not a new clip-length rule. After the clip exists, the loop brace and Duplicate can take it toward 16, not past it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sixteen bars is enough for a long progression or a beat that changes every four bars. It is not Arrangement View. Note still has no linear timeline. Structure lives in scenes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Instrument View, tap the plus control and look at the offered lengths: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16.","Create a 16-bar clip (or Duplicate a shorter clip until you reach 16).","Try to extend past 16 with <strong>Duplicate Loop</strong> or by dragging the brace. Confirm the clip will not grow further.","Open Set Settings and remember the other hard limits: 8 tracks, 8 scenes.","Play the 16-bar clip once through without stopping.","Crop or rebuild to a length you will actually use today (2, 4, or 8)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the maximum clip length in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16 bars.","Which Fixed Length sizes can you choose when adding a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars.","Does Free Length with transport stopped let you capture 16 bars in one take? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That path records up to eight bars, then you Capture. Sixteen is the clip ceiling after the clip exists."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, “What is the maximum clip length in Note?”","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 (Fixed Length 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16 bars); §8.1.2 (Free Length up to eight bars when stopped)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a 16-bar chord clip that changes harmony every four bars. Do not add a 17th bar. Launch it against a 1-bar drum loop from the previous lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-7","title":"6.2.7 — Only show selected loop-range contents","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.7 — Only show selected loop-range contents","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Frame the MIDI Editor on the looped region so you edit what actually repeats, not the unused bars outside the brace."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the loop is shorter than the clip, the brace is a window. <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong> (long-press, or swipe up on the brace) fits that window to the screen. Notes outside the brace stay in the clip; they just leave the frame. That is the documented way to “only show” the selected loop-range contents."},{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus cites a “1.4.3 Session tweak.” The local release-notes extract has no 1.4.3 line and no Session-View filter that hides non-looped clip contents. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for any 1.4.3 session-only behavior. Teach the manual’s zoom-to-loop behavior instead."},{"type":"prose","html":"After you zoom to the loop, pinch still works inside that frame. Swipe down or <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong> when you need to see the discarded-looking bars again — they are not discarded until you <strong>Crop</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when a Free Length take is eight bars and you only love bars 3–4. Brace those two bars, zoom to the loop, and edit them as if they were the whole clip. Decide later whether to Crop."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip whose loop brace is shorter than the clip.","Long-press the brace and choose <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong>, or swipe up on the brace.","Confirm the editor now frames only the looped bars.","Edit one note inside that frame.","Swipe down (or <strong>Zoom to Clip</strong>) and confirm the material outside the brace is still there.","Leave the clip uncropped unless you are ready to throw the outer bars away."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you make the editor show only the looped region? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Zoom to Loop</strong> from the brace menu, or swipe up on the brace.","Are notes outside the brace deleted by that zoom? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Only <strong>Crop</strong> discards them.","Is a 1.4.3 Session-View “show loop only” feature in the local extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.4 (Zoom to Loop; swipe up / swipe down)","Note Release Notes 1.4 (no 1.4.3 loop-range line in the local extract)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Take any clip longer than two bars. Loop only the best two bars, zoom to that loop, and tidy timing there. Zoom out and compare the unused bars before you crop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-2-8","title":"6.2.8 — Lab: crop a sloppy Free Length capture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.8 — Lab: crop a sloppy Free Length capture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.2 — Loop brace and clip structure</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn a messy Free Length take into a tight loop by moving the brace to the good bars and cropping everything else."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Free Length is how you capture without choosing a length first. With transport stopped you can play up to eight bars, then tap <strong>Capture</strong>. First capture with transport stopped also sets tempo. That path is generous. It is also how you end up with a pickup bar, a fumble, and two good bars in the middle."},{"type":"prose","html":"Repair is a loop-brace job, not a recapture. Play the clip. Drag or tap the brace so it covers only the bars you want to keep. Swipe up to zoom to that loop and listen until the downbeat feels like bar 1. Then long-press the brace and choose <strong>Crop</strong>. Clip length becomes the loop length. Content outside the brace is discarded."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the good idea is one bar inside an eight-bar blob, crop to one bar. If it is two bars, crop to two. You can <strong>Duplicate</strong> afterward to grow a clean 2-bar hook into 4. Crop first, duplicate second. Duplicating slop just makes longer slop."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not crop until the brace is right. <strong>Undo</strong> is there if you crop too soon. Note auto-saves every 10–15 seconds, so a crop you accept will stick."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["With transport stopped, play a sloppy phrase on pads (let it include a bad pickup). Tap <strong>Capture</strong>.","Open the MIDI Editor. Play the clip and mark the bars that actually work.","Resize the loop brace to those bars only. Swipe up and listen on repeat.","Long-press the brace and choose <strong>Crop</strong>.","Confirm the clip is now only the good length and the outer notes are gone.","Optional: <strong>Duplicate</strong> the clean loop once so you have room to vary the second half."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What must be true before <strong>Crop</strong> appears? <strong>Answer:</strong> Loop length is shorter than clip length.","What does <strong>Crop</strong> discard? <strong>Answer:</strong> Everything outside the loop brace.","Why crop a Free Length take instead of recapturing immediately? <strong>Answer:</strong> The good bars are already in the clip. Crop commits them without depending on a second performance."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 Free Length Capture; §8.2.4 Crop","Capture in Note (Free Length; transport stopped up to eight bars)","Note FAQ (10–15 second auto-save)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture three sloppy Free Length ideas. Crop each to the shortest loop that still feels like a part (1 or 2 bars). Keep the best one. Delete or ignore the others."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-2-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-6-3-103","code":"6.3","title":"6.3","blurb":"6.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l6-3-1","title":"6.3.1 — Tap grid to add (default one cell long)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.1 — Tap grid to add (default one cell long)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add a note by tapping an empty grid cell and know that a tap creates a note one cell long (or the same length as the preceding note)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap an empty location on the MIDI Editor grid to add a note. Row chooses the drum pad or pitch. Column chooses the time. By default, a newly tapped note is one grid cell long."},{"type":"prose","html":"The manual adds a second length rule: notes created by tapping inherit the same length as the preceding note. The first tap in a session is one cell. After you have drawn or stretched a longer note, the next tap follows that duration. If you need a different length on the next note, draw it (hold, then drag) instead of tapping. Drawing on a melodic clip is how you change duration for the next note you create."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tapping is the fastest way to punch a missing kick or a missing chord stab onto the grid after Capture. You do not need the transport running. You do not need to play the pad. The note appears where you tapped."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap an existing note and you delete it — that is the next lesson. Aim at empty cells when you mean to add."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip and the MIDI Editor. Expand if you need an empty row.","Tap one empty cell. Confirm a note appears and is one cell long.","Tap two more cells on different beats of the same row.","Play the clip and confirm the new notes sound.","Stretch one note longer (right edge), then tap a new cell and notice whether the new note matches that length.","Repeat the first-tap-on-a-fresh-clip case once from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you add a note in the MIDI Editor? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the grid on the desired row and timeline position.","How long is a newly tapped note by default? <strong>Answer:</strong> One grid cell. Tapped notes also follow the preceding note’s length.","When should you draw instead of tap on a melodic clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> When you want a different duration than the previous note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.1 Adding and Removing Notes","Note Release Notes 1.3 (tap add/delete)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a 1-bar drum clip, tap in a kick on 1 and 3 and a snare on 2 and 4 without playing the pads. Listen. Adjust only if a tap landed in the wrong cell."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-2","title":"6.3.2 — Tap note to delete","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.2 — Tap note to delete","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Delete a single note by tapping it, and use the note menu’s <strong>Delete</strong> when you need to remove a selection."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To delete a note, tap it. That is the whole single-note gesture. The note disappears. There is no trash can on the grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the inverse of tap-to-add, so aim matters. An empty cell adds. A filled cell deletes. If you meant to select the note instead, that is a <strong>long-press</strong>, not a tap."},{"type":"prose","html":"When several notes are selected, the <strong>Operations</strong> tab offers a <strong>Delete Notes</strong> button that removes the whole selection at once. After that batch delete, the note-editing menu closes automatically. Closing the editor or leaving Instrument View also clears selection, but it does not by itself delete notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use tap-delete to pull a flam, a doubled hat, or a bass mistake without recapturing. <strong>Undo</strong> brings the note back if your tap was sloppy."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that has at least four notes.","Tap one note you do not want. Confirm it is gone and the others remain.","<strong>Undo</strong>, then delete it again so the gesture is in your fingers.","Long-press to select two notes. Open <strong>Operations</strong> and tap <strong>Delete Notes</strong>.","Confirm both selected notes are gone and the menu closed.","Play the clip. If you deleted too much, <strong>Undo</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you delete one note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap it.","How do you delete several notes at once? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select them, then use <strong>Delete Notes</strong> on the <strong>Operations</strong> tab.","What happens to the note-editing menu after a batch delete? <strong>Answer:</strong> It closes automatically."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.1 (tap to delete); §8.2.3.1 Operations (Delete Notes; menu closes)","Note Release Notes 1.3 (tap add/delete)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a busy hat line, then tap-delete every other hat until the pattern breathes. Listen after each delete. Stop when the line is simpler, not empty."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-3","title":"6.3.3 — Move horizontally (time)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.3 — Move horizontally (time)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Move selected notes earlier or later on the timeline with a drag or with the <strong>Nudge</strong> buttons."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Time lives on the horizontal axis. After you select a note (long-press), tap the <strong>left edge or the center</strong> of a selected note and drag left or right. The note slides along the row. Pitch or drum pad does not change."},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Operations</strong> tab does the same job in steps. Beat-division buttons at the top set the nudge size. The <strong>Nudge Left</strong> and <strong>Nudge Right</strong> arrows move the selection by that value. Set the division to <strong>OFF</strong> and the notes move in fine, unquantized increments — useful when a hit is only slightly late and a full 16th jump would be too far."},{"type":"prose","html":"Turn <strong>Triplets</strong> on in that tab and a ¼ division nudges by one-third of a beat. That is how you walk a note onto a triplet grid without opening the <strong>Quantization</strong> tab."},{"type":"prose","html":"Moving time is the first repair after Capture: a kick that missed the downbeat, a snare that rushed, a bass note that should answer a bar later. Drag when you want to see the note travel. Nudge when you want a counted step."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Long-press one late note so it is selected.","Drag from its left edge or center to the previous beat. Listen.","Open <strong>Operations</strong>, pick a beat division, and use <strong>Nudge Left</strong> / <strong>Nudge Right</strong>.","Set the division to <strong>OFF</strong> and nudge again. Confirm the step is smaller.","Enable <strong>Triplets</strong> with ¼ selected and nudge once. Confirm the step is a third of a beat.","Play the clip and leave the note where it belongs."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you grab a selected note to move it in time? <strong>Answer:</strong> The left edge or the center, then drag left or right.","What do the <strong>Operations</strong> arrow buttons do? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Nudge Left</strong> / <strong>Nudge Right</strong> by the selected beat division (or fine steps when set to <strong>OFF</strong>).","What does <strong>Triplets</strong> plus ¼ do to nudge size? <strong>Answer:</strong> Notes nudge by one-third of a beat."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.1 Operations Tab; §8.2.3.6 Editing Notes with Gestures","Note Release Notes 1.3 (move)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Take a 1-bar drum clip and move only the snare later by one 16th, then back onto 2 and 4 using <strong>Nudge</strong>. Do not quantize the whole clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-4","title":"6.3.4 — Move vertically (pitch / drum row)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.4 — Move vertically (pitch / drum row)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drag notes up or down — or use the <strong>Transpose</strong> tab — and hear the difference between melodic pitch and Drum Rack pad rows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vertical motion is transpose. Select notes, then tap the <strong>left edge or center</strong> and drag up or down. On a Drift, Wavetable, or Melodic Sampler clip, the notes change pitch. If <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> is set, the <strong>Transpose</strong> buttons snap those notes to scale degrees."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a Drum Rack, vertical motion does <strong>not</strong> retune the sample. It moves the notes to different pads. Octave transpose buttons are disabled on drums. The kick note becomes a snare note if you drag it onto the snare row. That is a different instrument, not a pitched kick — unless you later use the <strong>Pitches</strong> tab (or 16 Pitches) to retune a hit."},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Transpose</strong> tab gives the same move with buttons: semitone arrows on the left, <strong>OCT</strong> up/down on the right (melodic only). Use buttons when you want a counted interval. Use the drag when you want to land on a visible row."},{"type":"prose","html":"Getting this wrong is how a drum fill “changes pitch” in conversation when it actually changed pads. Say “moved to another pad” for drums. Say “transposed” for melodic clips."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On a melodic clip, select one note and drag it up a row. Play. Confirm the pitch changed.","Open <strong>Transpose</strong> and move the same note by one semitone, then by one octave if <strong>OCT</strong> is enabled.","Open a Drum Rack clip. Select a kick note and drag it to the snare row.","Play. Confirm you now hear the snare sample, not a pitched kick.","Open <strong>Transpose</strong> on the drum selection and confirm <strong>OCT</strong> is disabled.","Drag the note back to the kick row."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you transpose with a gesture? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the left edge or center of a selected note and drag up or down.","What does transpose do on a Drum Rack? <strong>Answer:</strong> It moves notes to different sample pads. It does not change pitch. <strong>OCT</strong> is disabled.","When do melodic <strong>Transpose</strong> buttons snap to scale? <strong>Answer:</strong> When a key and scale are set in Set Settings."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.2 Transpose Tab; §8.2.3.6 Gestures","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.5 Pitches Tab (drum pitch is a separate control)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a 2-bar bass figure. Duplicate the clip (or the loop), then transpose only the second half up an octave with <strong>OCT</strong>. On drums, move one fill from hat to perc by dragging rows, not by using <strong>Pitches</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-5","title":"6.3.5 — Length handle right edge","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.5 — Length handle right edge","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Change how long a note lasts by dragging its right edge, or by using <strong>Shorten</strong> / <strong>Extend</strong> on the <strong>Operations</strong> tab."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duration lives on the right edge. Tap that edge of a note (or of one note in a selection) and drag left to shorten or right to lengthen. This is the same handle Note 1.3 calls the length handle. It is also how you fix a Melodic Sampler or Drift note that was captured too short to hear the release."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Operations</strong> does the job in steps. <strong>Shorten</strong> and <strong>Extend</strong> change duration by the beat division you selected in the top row. <strong>OFF</strong> again means fine, unquantized increments. <strong>Triplets</strong> uses the nearest triplet of that division."},{"type":"prose","html":"On drums, length still matters when the pad envelope is in <strong>Gate</strong> mode: the sample plays only while the note is held. In <strong>Trig</strong> mode the sample follows its own hold/decay, so stretching the MIDI note may not change what you hear. If a drum note “refuses” to get longer in the speakers, check the sampler envelope before you assume the handle is broken."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tapped new notes default to one cell (or the previous note’s length). Drawing on a melodic clip is the other way to set length as you create. This lesson is for notes that already exist."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a melodic note. Drag its right edge until it lasts a full beat. Play.","Drag the same edge back so the note is a 16th.","Open <strong>Operations</strong>, pick 1/16, and tap <strong>Extend</strong> twice.","Set the division to <strong>OFF</strong> and tap <strong>Shorten</strong> until the length is slightly off the grid.","On a Drum Rack clip, lengthen a hat and listen. If nothing changes, note whether that pad is <strong>Trig</strong> or <strong>Gate</strong>.","Leave one note obviously longer than its neighbors so you can see the handle’s result."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which part of the note do you drag to change length? <strong>Answer:</strong> The right edge.","Which <strong>Operations</strong> buttons change duration? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Shorten</strong> and <strong>Extend</strong>, by the selected beat division.","Why might a longer drum note not sound longer? <strong>Answer:</strong> In <strong>Trig</strong> mode the sample plays for its own hold/decay, regardless of when the note ends."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.1 (right-edge length); §8.2.3.1; §8.2.3.6","Ableton Note Manual §7.2.2 / §7.2.3 (Trig vs Gate)","Note Release Notes 1.3 (length handle); 1.0.10 (note length editing)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a bass clip, make every note in bar 1 short and every note in bar 2 long, using only the right-edge handle. Do not change pitch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-6","title":"6.3.6 — Velocity: drag vertically on right handle","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.6 — Velocity: drag vertically on right handle","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Change a note’s velocity from the slider near its right edge, and read velocity as color saturation."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Velocity has its own handle. After a note is selected, a vertical slider appears near the <strong>right edge</strong>. Drag that slider up to raise velocity, down to lower it. The note’s color saturation follows: higher saturation, higher velocity; lower saturation, lower velocity."},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Velocity</strong> tab shows the same values as a row of vertical sliders, one per selected note. Drag one slider, or swipe across several to make a smooth transition. Two fingers create a ramp between the leftmost and rightmost notes in the selection. Those batch moves belong with this handle: the tab and the on-note slider are the same parameter."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.3 lists “velocity on right handle” with tap add/delete and the length handle. Do not confuse the two right-side gestures. Horizontal drag on the right <strong>edge</strong> is length. Vertical drag on the <strong>velocity slider</strong> is velocity."},{"type":"prose","html":"On drums this is ghost notes versus backbeats. On a snare, low velocity plus <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> (a later instrument lesson) makes the same pad whisper. Even without that knob, many presets already respond to velocity."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Long-press a note so you can see the velocity slider near the right edge.","Drag the slider down. Confirm the color washes out and the note plays quieter (or thinner, depending on the preset).","Drag it up until the color is fully saturated.","Select four hats. Open the <strong>Velocity</strong> tab and swipe across the sliders to make a crescendo.","Undo, then use two fingers on the <strong>Velocity</strong> tab to ramp from the first hat to the last.","Play the clip and confirm you can hear the difference without looking."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you change velocity on the grid? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drag the velocity slider near the right edge of a selected note up or down.","How is velocity shown besides the slider? <strong>Answer:</strong> Color saturation — more saturation, higher velocity.","How is that different from the length handle? <strong>Answer:</strong> Length is a horizontal drag on the right edge. Velocity is a vertical drag on the slider."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.4 Velocity Tab; §8.2.3.6 Gestures","Note Release Notes 1.3 (velocity on right handle)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a 1-bar hat pattern, set beat 1 loud, the off-beats quiet, and beat 3 medium, using only the right-edge velocity slider. Do not quantize and do not change timing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-7","title":"6.3.7 — Long-press drag multi-select","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.7 — Long-press drag multi-select","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select one note, many notes, or a box of notes so the note-editing menu applies to the whole selection."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press a note to select it. Once one note is selected, drag in the editor to add more notes to the selection. You can also build a selection without starting on a note: tap and briefly hold in an empty area next to the notes, then immediately drag to draw a selection box."},{"type":"prose","html":"After a selection exists, tap additional individual notes to add them. Tap and drag again to start a <strong>new</strong> selection (that replaces the old box). To clear the selection, tap outside it or close the note-editing menu. Closing the MIDI Editor or leaving Instrument View also clears selection."},{"type":"prose","html":"The moment a selection exists, the note-editing menu opens under the editor. That is how you batch-nudge, transpose, quantize, or velocity-ramp a whole phrase. Capture in Note describes the same idea in older language: tap the mini preview, then drag in Clip View to select notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"A tap on a selected note is still dangerous if you are not careful — a plain tap on an unselected note deletes it. Select first (long-press), then drag. If you only needed one note, the long-press is enough."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Long-press one note. Confirm it is selected and the note-editing menu appeared.","Drag across neighboring notes so they join the selection.","Clear the selection by tapping empty grid space.","Tap and briefly hold next to a group of notes, then drag a box around them.","Tap one extra note outside the box to add it.","Close the menu (or tap outside) and confirm nothing is selected."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the gesture to select a single note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Long-press it.","How do you select several notes without first selecting one? <strong>Answer:</strong> Briefly hold in an empty area next to them, then drag a selection box.","Name two ways selection is cleared. <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap outside the selection or close the note-editing menu. Also: close the editor or leave Instrument View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.2 Selecting Notes","Note Release Notes 1.3 (long-press select)","Capture in Note, Editing Notes"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select every snare in a 2-bar clip with a box. Lower all of their velocities together. Clear the selection and confirm the kicks did not change."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-8","title":"6.3.8 — Note Properties menu batch ops","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.8 — Note Properties menu batch ops","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the four (or five) tabs of the note-editing menu to change a whole selection at once."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select notes and the note-editing menu opens below the MIDI Editor. It has four tabs on every clip: <strong>Operations</strong>, <strong>Transpose</strong>, <strong>Quantization</strong>, and <strong>Velocity</strong>. Drum clips add a fifth tab, <strong>Pitches</strong>."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Operations</strong> — beat-division row (including <strong>OFF</strong> and <strong>Triplets</strong>), <strong>Shorten</strong> / <strong>Extend</strong>, <strong>Nudge Left</strong> / <strong>Nudge Right</strong>, <strong>Delete Notes</strong>.","<strong>Transpose</strong> — semitone arrows; <strong>OCT</strong> up/down on melodic clips. Drums move to other pads; <strong>OCT</strong> is disabled. Melodic transpose snaps to scale if Key &amp; Scale is set.","<strong>Quantization</strong> — tap a meter value to snap the selection; <strong>OFF</strong> returns the selection to its original positions; <strong>Triplets</strong> uses the nearest triplet of that value.","<strong>Velocity</strong> — per-note sliders; swipe for a curve; two fingers for a ramp. Saturation on the notes mirrors the values.","<strong>Pitches</strong> (drums only) — per-hit pitch sliders. This is the tab that retunes a kick or tom. It is not the same as <strong>Transpose</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture in Note’s older article names three tabs (Operations, Quantization, Velocity) and folds transpose into Operations. The manual is the complete current set. Use the manual’s five-tab picture."},{"type":"prose","html":"Full screen also exposes the menu from the bottom-bar icon if you want the tabs without a selection already visible. Selecting notes is still the reliable way to know which events you are changing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a bar of notes. Name each tab as you open it.","On <strong>Operations</strong>, nudge the selection by 1/16, then <strong>Undo</strong>.","On <strong>Transpose</strong>, move a melodic selection one scale step (or a drum selection one pad).","On <strong>Quantization</strong>, tap 1/16, listen, then tap <strong>OFF</strong> to hear the original timing return.","On <strong>Velocity</strong>, raise the whole selection slightly.","If this is a drum clip, open <strong>Pitches</strong> and move one hit, then put it back."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which tabs exist on every clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Operations</strong>, <strong>Transpose</strong>, <strong>Quantization</strong>, <strong>Velocity</strong>.","Which extra tab do drum clips get, and what does it change? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pitches</strong> — the pitch of individual drum hits, not the pad row.","What does <strong>Quantization</strong> → <strong>OFF</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It returns the selection to its original positions on the grid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3 through §8.2.3.5","Capture in Note, Editing Notes (older three-tab wording)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select a whole 2-bar melody. Quantize to 1/16, then use <strong>Velocity</strong> to fade the phrase down. Do not delete anything. Play before-and-after with <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-9","title":"6.3.9 — Paint consecutive drum notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.9 — Paint consecutive drum notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Draw on a drum clip to create a run of consecutive notes on one pad row."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap-and-hold on the MIDI Editor until a note appears, then drag left or right. That is drawing. On a <strong>drum</strong> clip, the drag creates <strong>multiple consecutive notes</strong> along the row. You are painting a roll or a 16th hat line, not stretching one long note."},{"type":"prose","html":"That difference is the whole lesson. Drums treat the draw as a series of hits. Melodic clips treat the same gesture as one note’s length (next lesson). If you draw across a kick row, you get a stream of kicks. If you wanted one long kick sample, you would tap once and then drag the right-edge length handle — and even then <strong>Trig</strong> vs <strong>Gate</strong> decides what you hear."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drawing follows the current grid, including <strong>Triplets</strong> if that toggle is on. Zoom in first if the cells are too small to control. You can still tap-delete individual hits from the painted run."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this for hats, shakers, and percussion rolls that are faster than you want to play. Capture the groove idea if you can play it; paint the mechanical 16ths you do not want to finger."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drum Rack clip and expand the editor so the hat row is visible.","Tap and hold on an empty hat cell until a note appears, then drag to the right across one bar.","Confirm you created many short consecutive notes, not one long bar-length note.","Play. If the line is too dense, tap-delete every other hit.","Turn <strong>Triplets</strong> on and paint one beat of consecutive notes. Listen to the different spacing.","Repeat the draw on a perc row from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does drawing (hold, then drag) create on a drum clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Multiple consecutive notes on that row.","How is that different from drawing on a melodic clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Melodic drawing shortens or extends a single note’s length.","If you need one long drum sample instead of a roll, what should you not do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Do not paint. Tap once, then use the length handle — and check <strong>Trig</strong> / <strong>Gate</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.1 Adding and Removing Notes (drum draw = consecutive notes)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Paint a 1-bar closed-hat 16th line. Delete the hits on beats 2 and 4. You now have a pattern you could not have played as cleanly at tempo."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-10","title":"6.3.10 — Paint variable-length melodic notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.10 — Paint variable-length melodic notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Draw on a melodic clip to create one note and set its duration in the same gesture."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The draw gesture is the same hold-then-drag. On a <strong>melodic</strong> clip (Drift, Wavetable, Melodic Sampler) it does not stamp a row of notes. It creates <strong>one</strong> note and shortens or extends that note as you drag."},{"type":"prose","html":"The manual’s reason: tapped notes inherit the preceding note’s length. If the last note you made was a 16th, the next tap is a 16th. Drawing is how you break that inheritance and set a new duration for the note you are creating — and therefore for the taps that follow."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this for bass that needs a whole-note under a moving drum loop, or a stab that must stay short. After the note exists, the right-edge handle still edits length. Drawing is the create-time version of that handle."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not paint a scale run by dragging horizontally on one row. That would be one long note. To write a run, tap (or draw) each pitch on its own row, or play the pads and Capture."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drift, Wavetable, or Melodic Sampler clip.","Tap and hold on a pitch row until a note appears, then drag right across two beats.","Confirm you have one long note, not a series of cells.","Draw a second note that is only one 16th long on another beat.","Tap an empty cell after that short note. Confirm the new tap follows the short length.","Play the clip and name which notes you drew versus tapped."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does drawing create on a melodic clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> One note, with its length set by the drag.","Why does the manual recommend drawing on melodic clips? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tapped notes copy the preceding note’s length. Drawing lets you change duration for the next note.","Can you paint a melody by dragging across one row? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That only lengthens one pitch. Change rows (or play pads) for new pitches."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.1 Adding and Removing Notes (melodic draw = length)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a 2-bar bass line using only drawing for the long notes and taps for the short notes. Do not use the right-edge handle until the last listen, and then only if a length is still wrong."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-11","title":"6.3.11 — Newly added notes and overwrite behavior","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.11 — Newly added notes and overwrite behavior","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State what the sources actually say about new notes — length inheritance — and treat “overwrite” as a gap unless you observe it on your device."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The syllabus asks for “newly added notes and overwrite behavior (RN evolution).” The local release-notes extract does not describe overwrite, stacking, or replacement when a new note lands on a cell that already has a note. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for any official overwrite rule."},{"type":"prose","html":"What the manual <em>does</em> document for newly added notes:"},{"type":"list","items":["A tap creates a note one grid cell long by default.","Tapped notes take the same length as the preceding note.","Drawing on drums creates consecutive notes; drawing on melodic clips sets one note’s length.","A tap on an existing note deletes it. That is not overwrite. That is delete-then-you-can-tap-again-to-add."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"So the safe practice is: if a cell is already occupied, tap deletes. Add the replacement on the empty cell. Do not assume a second add will replace velocity, pitch, or length in place."},{"type":"prose","html":"If your installed Note does something else — stacks two notes, replaces velocity, refuses the add — write the version from <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and describe the trial. Do not turn that observation into a universal rule in later lessons."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Tap a note onto an empty cell. Note its length.","Stretch that note, then tap a new cell. Confirm the new note inherited the length.","Tap the new note (delete). Tap the same cell again (add). Confirm you did delete-then-add, not an in-place overwrite.","Try adding on top of an existing note without deleting first. Write what happens on <em>your</em> device.","Draw a short note, then tap. Confirm length inheritance again.","Record any overwrite-like behavior as a versioned observation, not as a manual fact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What length do newly tapped notes use? <strong>Answer:</strong> One cell by default, then the same length as the preceding note.","What does a tap on an existing note do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Deletes it.","Is overwrite-on-add documented in the local extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.1 Adding and Removing Notes","Note Release Notes 1.3 (tap add/delete; no overwrite line in the extract)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Rebuild a 1-bar bass clip by deleting every note and tapping them back in a new rhythm. Use inheritance: draw one long note, then tap the remaining long notes; draw one short note, then tap the stabs."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-12","title":"6.3.12 — 16 Pitches: change drum note pitch via properties","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.12 — 16 Pitches: change drum note pitch via properties","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Retune individual drum hits with the <strong>Pitches</strong> tab, and keep that separate from <strong>Transpose</strong> (which changes pads)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drum clips have an extra note-property tab: <strong>Pitches</strong>. Select hits, open <strong>Pitches</strong>, and drag the vertical sliders to raise or lower each hit’s pitch. Swipe across the sliders to make a smooth pitch transition, the same gesture family as velocity ramps."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the editor’s way to tune a kick, slide a tom fill, or add hat pitch motion. Beatmaking with Ableton Note calls hat pitch modulation extra style. The <strong>Pitches</strong> tab is the control that does it after Capture."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Transpose</strong> on drums is a different operation. It moves notes to other pads. Octave buttons are disabled. If you want the same sample at a new pitch, stay on <strong>Pitches</strong>. If you want a different sample, use <strong>Transpose</strong> or drag to another row."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.3 also adds <strong>16 Pitches</strong> as a drum performance option (play one sample at sixteen pitches on the pads). That is a playing layout, documented in the release notes, not spelled out in the local manual’s pad chapter. The editor tab still retunes notes you already captured, whether you played them on 16 Pitches or on the 4×4 grid. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for the exact 16 Pitches pad-layout UI beyond the release-note name."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Drum Rack clip. Select one kick.","Open <strong>Transpose</strong> and move it one step. Play. Confirm you hear a different pad.","<strong>Undo</strong>. Open <strong>Pitches</strong> and raise the same kick. Play. Confirm it is still the kick sample, retuned.","Select a run of hats. Swipe across <strong>Pitches</strong> sliders to make a short pitch fall.","If your pad-options sidebar shows <strong>16 Pitches</strong>, play a fill there, Capture, and inspect those notes in <strong>Pitches</strong>.","Return any experimental pitch to a useful tuning."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which tab retunes drum hits without changing pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pitches</strong>.","What does <strong>Transpose</strong> do on drums? <strong>Answer:</strong> Moves notes to different sample pads. It does not change pitch. <strong>OCT</strong> is disabled.","What is <strong>16 Pitches</strong> in the 1.3 release notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> A drum-note pitch feature that arrived with the MIDI Editor pack. Exact pad UI is not in the local manual extract."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.2 Transpose; §8.2.3.5 Pitches Tab","Note Release Notes 1.3 (16 Pitches for drum notes)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (hat pitch modulation)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tune one kick so it sits under your bass root (by ear). Add a three-note tom or hat pitch fall using only <strong>Pitches</strong>. Do not transpose those notes to other pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-13","title":"6.3.13 — Audio preview while editing when stopped","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.13 — Audio preview while editing when stopped","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Edit notes with the transport stopped and hear each touch thanks to the 1.5 editor preview."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.5 adds audio preview when you touch-edit notes while transport is stopped. You do not have to press Play to know what a moved snare or a new bass note sounds like. Touch the note — move it, draw it, change length or velocity — and the app sounds the result."},{"type":"prose","html":"Before 1.5, the grid was silent unless the clip was running. If your install is older, update, or keep Play running while you edit. Check the version in <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Preview is not the same as launching the clip. It does not start the scene or the global transport. It is a momentary sound for the note you are touching. Launch the clip afterward to hear the line in context with other tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when you are drawing a bass figure in a quiet room or repairing hats on headphones without looping the whole beat for ten minutes. Wired headphones still have less latency than Bluetooth (34–200 ms Bluetooth vs 5–10 ms wired). Preview does not change that."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note is 1.5 or later (<strong>Settings → Help</strong>).","Stop the transport. Open the MIDI Editor on a drum clip.","Tap-add one note. Confirm you hear the pad without Play running.","Drag that note to another time and to another row. Confirm each touch previews.","Change its velocity slider. Listen for the quieter or louder preview.","Press Play and hear the same note in the loop."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version previews notes while transport is stopped? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.5.","Does that preview start the Set’s transport? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is audio preview of the touch-edit, not a clip launch.","What should you do if stopped edits are silent? <strong>Answer:</strong> Check that you are on 1.5 or later, or press Play while editing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.5 (MIDI Editor: audio preview when touch-editing notes while transport is stopped)","Bluetooth Headphones and Latency in Note (wired 5–10 ms; Bluetooth 34–200 ms)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With transport stopped, draw a 1-bar bass line entirely by preview. Only press Play once, at the end, against your drum clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-14","title":"6.3.14 — Triplets grid toggle","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.14 — Triplets grid toggle","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn the editor’s <strong>Triplets</strong> grid on when you need to place, nudge, or quantize notes on triplet subdivisions."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Triplets</strong> appears in more than one place, and the places do related jobs:"},{"type":"list","items":["Full-screen bottom menu: <strong>Triplets</strong> changes the <strong>grid spacing</strong> in the editor so columns sit on triplet values.","<strong>Operations</strong> tab: <strong>Triplets</strong> uses the nearest triplet of the selected beat division. Example from the manual: Triplets + ¼ makes <strong>Nudge</strong> move notes by one-third of a beat.","<strong>Quantization</strong> tab: <strong>Triplets</strong> uses the nearest triplet of the meter value you tap."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Note Repeat already offers 1/4T, 1/8T, 1/16T, and 1/32T while you play. The editor toggle is how you see and repair those notes after Capture. If you captured a triplet hat with Repeat and the straight grid makes every note look “off,” turn <strong>Triplets</strong> on before you start dragging."},{"type":"prose","html":"Turning the grid to triplets does not by itself rewrite existing notes. It changes where new taps land and how the Operations/Quantize math counts. Quantize to a triplet value when you intend to snap. Leave notes alone when the slightly-off placement is the feel."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Expand the MIDI Editor. Tap <strong>Triplets</strong> in the bottom menu. Confirm the grid columns change.","Tap-add a short run of notes. Confirm they sit on the triplet grid.","Turn <strong>Triplets</strong> off. Confirm the same notes now look off-center on the straight grid.","Select those notes. In <strong>Quantization</strong>, enable <strong>Triplets</strong> and tap a value (for example 1/8). Listen.","In <strong>Operations</strong>, enable <strong>Triplets</strong>, select ¼, and nudge once. Confirm the step is a third of a beat.","Leave <strong>Triplets</strong> off unless the next phrase needs it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does full-screen <strong>Triplets</strong> change? <strong>Answer:</strong> The editor’s grid spacing, to triplets.","What does <strong>Operations</strong> Triplets + ¼ do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Nudges (and length steps) by one-third of a beat.","Does enabling the grid rewrite notes by itself? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It changes placement and the math for nudge/quantize. Notes move when you add, nudge, or quantize."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 (Triplets icon); §8.2.3.1; §8.2.3.3","Note Release Notes 1.3 (Triplets toggle)","Capture in Note / Manual §8.1.3 (Repeat includes triplet divisions)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture a 1-bar hat using Repeat at 1/8T. Open the editor, turn <strong>Triplets</strong> on, and delete one note per beat to make a skip. Do not quantize to straight 1/16."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-14</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-15","title":"6.3.15 — Quantize from editor / Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.15 — Quantize from editor / Instrument View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize a selection from the editor tab, or all notes from the Instrument View <strong>Note Quantize</strong> icon, and know how to undo a snap that kills the groove."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Two official paths:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Quantization</strong> tab in the note-editing menu. Select notes, tap a meter value, and those notes snap to that grid. <strong>OFF</strong> returns the selection to its original positions. <strong>Triplets</strong> uses the nearest triplet of the value. The tab also lets you specify <strong>how much</strong> they snap — the same idea Note 1.0.15 lists as quantisation amount."],"ordered":true},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Note Quantize</strong> icon in the Instrument View bottom row. Tap once to quantize <strong>all</strong> notes using the current settings. Long-press the icon to open the Quantization tab and change those settings. That long-press <strong>selects all notes in the clip</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize is a repair tool, not a default. Capture already put timing into the clip. Full snap is right when a kick must lock to a click. It is wrong when the late hat <em>is</em> the feel. Groove Amount (Set Settings, 1.0.15) and per-clip swing are the other feel controls — do not fight them with a 100 percent 1/16 quantize."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you snap too hard, use <strong>OFF</strong> on the selection (returns original positions) or <strong>Undo</strong>. Then nudge only the notes that are actually late."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Capture a slightly messy 1-bar drum loop on purpose.","Select only the kicks. In <strong>Quantization</strong>, tap 1/4. Listen. Confirm hats did not move.","Tap <strong>OFF</strong> on that selection and confirm the kicks return.","Leave the editor. Tap the Instrument View <strong>Note Quantize</strong> icon once. Confirm the whole clip snaps.","<strong>Undo</strong>. Long-press the same icon, change the value or amount, then apply.","Keep the version (partial or full) that still feels like a beat."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a single tap on the Instrument View <strong>Note Quantize</strong> icon do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Quantizes all notes in the clip to the current quantization settings.","What extra thing does a long-press on that icon do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Opens the Quantization tab and selects all notes in the clip.","What does <strong>OFF</strong> in the Quantization tab do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Returns the selection to its original positions on the grid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (Note Quantize button); §8.2.3.3 Quantization Tab","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Quantisation amount)","Capture in Note, Editing Notes"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize only kicks and snares in a 2-bar loop. Leave hats unquantized. That split is the lesson: lock the skeleton, keep the ride human."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-15</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-16","title":"6.3.16 — Lab: humanize via velocity only","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.16 — Lab: humanize via velocity only","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make a mechanical line feel played by changing only velocity — not timing, not pitch, not a fictional Humanize button."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note has no documented “Humanize” command. <code>{type:'gap'}</code> for any one-tap humanize. What you do have is velocity."},{"type":"prose","html":"Color saturation shows how hard each note is. The right-edge slider and the <strong>Velocity</strong> tab change that value. Swipe across sliders for a curve. Two fingers ramp from the first selected note to the last. That is enough to turn a painted 16th hat line into something that rises into the backbeat and falls away."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep timing frozen. Do not nudge. Do not quantize. Do not touch <strong>Pitches</strong>. If the line still sounds like a machine after the velocities are different, that is useful information: some presets barely respond to velocity. Drum Sampler’s <strong>Vel &gt; Vol</strong> (Level 7) is the device-side partner to this lab. For this lesson, pick a kit that already gets quieter when you play softer on the pads, then edit those values on the grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking with Ableton Note treats hats as the place you “skip through note divisions to tell a story.” Velocity is the other half of that story: which of those divisions speak up."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Paint or Repeat-capture a straight 1-bar 16th hat line. Leave timing alone.","Select all hats. Set them to a medium velocity so you have room up and down.","Raise the hats that sit on beats 1 and 3. Lower the ones on the “e” and “a.”","Use a two-finger ramp on one beat to swell into the next downbeat.","Play with drums and bass. If you cannot hear the difference, switch kits and repeat.","<strong>Undo</strong> once to compare machine vs shaped, then <strong>Redo</strong> and keep the shaped version."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is there a Humanize button in the extracts? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. <code>{type:'gap'}</code>. You shape feel with velocity (and, in other lessons, groove/nudge).","How do you make a smooth velocity change across a selection? <strong>Answer:</strong> Swipe across the <strong>Velocity</strong> sliders, or use two fingers to ramp from the leftmost to the rightmost note.","What must you not change in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> Timing, length, and pitch."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.4 Velocity Tab","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (hats as storytelling; delay-before-judge)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Five to ten minutes: one hat pattern, three velocity passes (quiet, then accent 2 and 4, then a one-bar crescendo). Keep the pass you still like after a short break."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-16</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-17","title":"6.3.17 — Lab: fix timing without full quantize","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.17 — Lab: fix timing without full quantize","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Repair only the notes that are actually late or early, using nudge and partial snap, without flattening the whole clip to the grid."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Full-clip quantize is the blunt tool. This lab uses the fine tools."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Operations</strong> → beat division <strong>OFF</strong> nudges in unquantized increments. That is how you walk a kick a few milliseconds without jumping a full 16th. A larger division (1/16, 1/32) is for notes that belong on a different cell. <strong>Triplets</strong> is for notes that belong on a triplet grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Quantization</strong> tab’s amount control (manual: “how much they are snapped”; RN 1.0.15: quantisation amount) lets you pull a selection toward the grid without gluing it there. Select only the offending notes. Leave the hats that make the groove."},{"type":"prose","html":"Instrument View <strong>Note Quantize</strong> selects and processes the <strong>entire</strong> clip. Do not tap it in this lab. If you do, <strong>Undo</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Musical test: if the repaired loop could be a different take of the same idea, you succeeded. If it could be a MIDI file exported from a piano roll set to 100 percent snap, you went too far. (Note cannot export MIDI files anyway — the comparison is only a feel check.)"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Capture a 2-bar drum or bass take with the metronome on. Keep a couple of late notes.","Play and mark only the notes that bother you.","Select one late note. Set <strong>Operations</strong> to <strong>OFF</strong> and nudge until it sits.","Select a note that is a full cell off. Nudge by 1/16 or 1/32.","If a group is slightly rushed, select just that group and apply a partial quantize amount — not a full snap.","Play against the metronome, then with the metronome off. Keep the version that still has feel."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does <strong>Operations</strong> set to <strong>OFF</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Adjusts the selection in fine, unquantized increments.","Why avoid the Instrument View <strong>Note Quantize</strong> tap in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> It quantizes all notes in the clip.","What is the success test? <strong>Answer:</strong> Only the problem notes moved. The groove of the rest is intact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.1 Operations; §8.2.3.3 Quantization","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Quantisation amount)","Note FAQ (no MIDI import/export)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Repair one Gate E capture (drums or bass) using only nudge and partial quantize. Do not touch velocity in this pass. Save the clip. Gate F will ask for both timing and velocity next."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-17</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l6-3-18","title":"6.3.18 — Gate F: edit the Gate E captures","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.18 — Gate F: edit the Gate E captures","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 6 · Module 6.3 — Adding, selecting, transforming notes</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Score your own Set against the Gate F checklist: the Gate E captures now have repaired time, pitch/row, length, velocity, and loop length."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate F is not a new feature. It is a self-scored repair of the drums, bass, and melody you captured for Gate E. Use only facts from this level. There is no in-app grader."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass each item on your own Set (not a Demo):</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["You can open the MIDI Editor by tapping <strong>Clip Preview</strong>, expand to full screen (and landscape), pinch-zoom, and close via Track Preset tabs.","Loop brace: you can resize, <strong>Duplicate</strong>, <strong>Crop</strong>, and zoom (swipe up/down). No clip exceeds 16 bars. Uneven lengths across tracks are allowed.","You can tap-add, tap-delete, draw (consecutive notes on drums, length on melodic), long-press select, and use <strong>Operations</strong> / <strong>Transpose</strong> / <strong>Quantization</strong> / <strong>Velocity</strong> / <strong>Pitches</strong>.","Drum <strong>Transpose</strong> moves pad rows. Drum <strong>Pitches</strong> retunes hits. You did not confuse the two.","Timing repairs used nudge or partial quantize with a reason. You can say why any full quantize was applied.","Velocity on at least one part was shaped on purpose (accents, ghosts, or a ramp).","At least one sloppy Free Length take was cropped to the good bars."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Fail any line, return to that lesson, then re-score. Auto-save will keep the repaired Set (every 10–15 seconds). Do not start Level 7 until every required line is a pass."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Gate E Set (drums + bass + melody captured).","Walk the checklist above. Write pass or fail next to each line.","Repair every fail in the MIDI Editor. Play the scene after each repair.","Confirm clip lengths and loops on all three tracks. Crop or duplicate where the form needs it.","Listen once all the way through without touching the screen.","Re-score. Repeat failed items only. Stop when the list is all pass."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What Set do you score? <strong>Answer:</strong> Your own Gate E captures, not a Demo Set.","What does “quantize with a reason” mean here? <strong>Answer:</strong> You can name why those notes were snapped (and you left other notes alone if they carried the feel).","May you start Level 7 with a failed line? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Re-score until every required item passes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 entire (editor, notes, loop brace)","Note FAQ (16-bar clips; 8 tracks; 8 scenes)","Note Release Notes 1.3, 1.5, 1.0.15"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the pass/fail list in a note or in the Set name. Keep the repaired Set. That file is the Gate F portfolio."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l6-3-18</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
